.TH CCINIT 1 "2026-07-17" "codecommit-utils" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccinit \- create an AWS CodeCommit repository and seed it with a README
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccinit
.I repository\-name
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ccinit
creates the named CodeCommit repository, clones it over HTTPS into the current
directory, writes a placeholder
.BR README.md ,
then commits and pushes it. The result is a repository with an initial commit
rather than an empty one.
.PP
It is a thin wrapper around
.BR cch (1),
which holds the implementation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccinit \fIrepository\-name\fR    is  cch repo create \fIrepository\-name\fR \-\-readme
.fi
.RE
.PP
Both spellings are fully supported.
.BR cch (1)
is the recommended entry point for new work, and additionally offers
.B cch repo create
without
.BR \-\-readme ,
which creates an empty repository and does not clone it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AWS_PROFILE
The AWS profile whose credentials and region are used, as for the AWS CLI.
.TP
.B AWS_REGION
The AWS region to operate in, as for the AWS CLI.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exits 0 on success, and 1 when no repository name is supplied. Failures
reported by the AWS CLI or by
.BR git (1)
propagate their own exit status.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cch (1),
.BR ccclone (1),
.BR cclist (1),
.BR git (1),
.BR aws (1)
